{
  "summary": "One finding needs your attention soon. A scheduled task called \\TelemetryRefresh is set to download text from the internet and run it as commands, which is a pattern malware often uses. The second finding is a routine reminder to confirm you recognize an extra administrator account. Look at the scheduled task first, then check the account.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Scheduled task that downloads and runs commands from the internet",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PERSIST-SUSPICIOUS:2041796b"
      ],
      "explanation": "A scheduled task named \\TelemetryRefresh is in the Ready state, so it can run on its own. Its command uses curl to quietly fetch a file from a web address (cdn.example.invalid/t.txt) and then passes whatever that file contains straight to the Windows command prompt to run. Whoever controls that web address could change what your computer does each time the task runs. The task was created under your own account (<user>) and runs with limited rights, not full administrator rights. That limits the damage somewhat, but it can still reach your files and your account. Some genuine software uses scheduled tasks, but downloading commands and running them blindly is a common malware technique. If you didn't set this up and don't recognize it, treat it as a serious concern and follow the fix shown for this finding in the report."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Extra administrator account to confirm",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "There is a local account, <computer>\\<account-2>, with full administrator rights. That means it can change anything on the computer. Having a separate administrator account is often normal and even good practice, so this is rated low. Just make sure you know what this account is and who uses it. An administrator account you don't recognize can be a sign of a break-in. That matters more here because a suspicious task was also found, so it's worth a quick check. This was a first scan, so the report can't say whether the account is new."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": []
}
